  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure currently resides over the eastern CONUS\nwith broad southwesterly flow aloft; a similar forecast to the\npast week. As such, the forecast remains dry and slightly above\naverage with highs in the upper 70s and low 80s. Once again,\ntoday's NBM data is running a bit too humid. So, we have lowered\ndewpoints slightly across the region into the middle to upper 40s.\nThis results in minimum relative humidity values falling towards\n30 percent with some areas dropping into the 20s. This will lead\nto a continuation of the elevated fire danger across the region.\n\nNot much will change in terms of the surface or upper pattern\nthrough this Friday. Expect dry weather and highs in the low 80s\nwith elevated fire danger each afternoon. By late Friday and into\nSaturday, a belt of stronger upper winds associated with the\nSubtropical Jet Stream will overspread the southern CONUS. A\ntrough will develop along the exit region of the jet, with a mild\nmass response across the Mid-South. Southerly flow will advect\nlow-level humidity back into the region, driving higher precipitation\nchances into this weekend. However, the parent upper trough and \nassociated features near the surface will be relatively weak, \nleading to QPF values generally below 1.00\" with the best rainfall\nchances across west Tennessee and northern Mississippi. A few \nrumbles of thunder may occur Sunday, but thunder chances remain at\nor below 15%.\n\nAs we move into next week, ridging will return Monday behind the \nleading wave that will bring rain showers back to the forecast \nthis weekend. Another wave will then drop southeast into the \nPlains, eventually phasing with the belt of southwesterlies over \nthe southern Rockies. Ensembles and deterministic guidance both \ndepict the outcome of the phasing to be a cutoff low developing \nover the central CONUS to the west of the Mid-South. Further \nmoisture advection is expected ahead of the low, leading to more \nprecipitation chances into the middle of next week and the end of \nthe forecast. But forecast uncertainty remains high, typical of \ncutoff systems, with changes in the amount of rainfall \naccumulation expected in the coming days.",
